export const FS1={title:'The event, forensic history and careers',hook:'Forensic science applies scientific methods to questions of law. A fingerprint on a glass, a fiber on a jacket or the temperature of a body can help answer what happened, when and how. HOSA’s Forensic Science event asks two-person teams to master this knowledge on a written test and then analyze a death case like a medical examiner’s team.',objectives:['Describe the Forensic Science event, test plan and case study format.','Identify key figures and milestones in forensic history.','Distinguish medical examiners and coroners.','Describe forensic science careers and education paths.'],sections:[
['The event at a glance',[
'Forensic Science is a **team event for two members** intended for advanced students who have studied **medical terminology** and **anatomy and physiology**. **Round One** is a **50-question test in 60 minutes**. The **team average** qualifies teams for Round Two and is **added to the case study score** for final results; it also breaks ties.',
'In **Round Two**, teams analyze the same **secret case study**—a physical scene, a written scenario or a digital format, often with a **police report** and **autopsy** information—and have **40 minutes** to write a conclusion, plus **five minutes** to upload it as a **PDF**. The conclusion states the **time of death range**, **immediate cause of death**, **manner of death**, **other contributing conditions** and the **evidence** supporting each. Teams bring a charged device with a typing program, internet access and a USB port; electricity is not provided, and internet research is not allowed.',
'The guidelines caution that case materials can be graphic, and advisors should seek approval before students participate.',
{t:'table',caption:'Written test plan',head:['Area','Weight'],rows:[['Crime Scene and Death Investigation','20%'],['Forensic Toxicology and Pharmacology','20%'],['Forensic History and Careers','10%'],['Forensic Anthropology','10%'],['Forensic Entomology','10%'],['Blood, Bloodstains, Biological Fluids and Stains','10%'],['Techniques of DNA Analysis','10%'],['Forensic Psychology and Psychiatry','10%']]}]],
['The case study rubric',[
'The written conclusion is scored out of **80 points**, with eight items worth 10 points each: **time of death range**, **immediate cause of death**, **manner of death** (natural, accident, suicide, homicide or undetermined), **other contributing conditions**, **connecting evidence to conclusions**, **application of anatomy, physiology and forensic knowledge**, **clear, concise and logical explanation** of physical evidence and **grammar, mechanics and spelling**.',
'Most items are scored as full credit, partial credit or none, so accuracy matters. The **evidence** items reward teams that cite **numerous specific facts** from the case—temperatures, stomach contents, injuries, toxicology—and explain their meaning using science.',
'Official test references are **Forensic Science Fundamentals** (Bertino and Bertino) and **Forensic Science** (Bell). DHO Health Science, medical terminology and disease texts are recommended for the case study but are not test sources.',
match('Conclusion section and what it states',[['Time of death range','Date and approximate time window'],['Immediate cause of death','The medical cause, such as a gunshot wound to the chest'],['Manner of death','Natural, accident, suicide, homicide or undetermined'],['Contributing conditions','Factors that contributed but did not directly cause death'],['Evidence','Facts supporting each conclusion'],['Mechanics','Grammar and spelling']])]],
['Milestones in forensic history',[
'Forensic science grew from many contributions. **Edmond Locard** (early 1900s) stated the **exchange principle**: every contact leaves a trace. **Alphonse Bertillon** created anthropometry, a body-measurement identification system, later replaced by **fingerprints**, whose classification was advanced by **Francis Galton** and **Edward Henry**. **Mathieu Orfila** is called the father of **toxicology**. **Karl Landsteiner** discovered **ABO blood groups** (1901).',
'**Calvin Goddard** advanced **firearms comparison** with the comparison microscope. **Alec Jeffreys** developed **DNA fingerprinting** in the mid-1980s, transforming identification. Fiction also shaped the field: Sir Arthur Conan Doyle’s Sherlock Holmes popularized scientific observation.',
'Test questions often match a person to a contribution.',
"Modern forensic science also relies on standards organizations and research agencies, such as the National Institute of Standards and Technology and the National Institute of Justice, which fund research, develop standards and help laboratories improve accuracy.",
match('Pioneer and contribution',[['Edmond Locard','Exchange principle'],['Mathieu Orfila','Father of toxicology'],['Karl Landsteiner','ABO blood groups'],['Alphonse Bertillon','Anthropometry'],['Calvin Goddard','Firearms comparison'],['Alec Jeffreys','DNA fingerprinting']])]],
['Medical examiners and coroners',[
'Deaths that are sudden, unexpected, violent or suspicious are investigated by a **medical examiner** or **coroner**, depending on the jurisdiction. A **medical examiner** is usually a physician—often a **forensic pathologist**—appointed to investigate deaths and perform autopsies. A **coroner** is often an **elected official** who may not be a physician and may hire pathologists to perform autopsies.',
'Their job is to determine the **cause** and **manner** of death and complete the death certificate. They work with law enforcement, crime scene investigators, toxicologists and other specialists.',
'In Round Two, your team plays a role similar to the medical examiner’s, drawing conclusions from scene and autopsy evidence.',
sort('Medical examiner or coroner?',['Medical examiner','Coroner'],[['Usually a physician',0],['Often elected',1],['Often a forensic pathologist',0],['May not have medical training',1],['Appointed to investigate deaths',0]])]],
['Forensic careers',[
'Forensic careers span many specialties: **forensic pathologist** (physician who performs autopsies), **crime scene investigator**, **forensic science technician**, **forensic toxicologist**, **forensic odontologist** (teeth and bite marks), **forensic anthropologist** (skeletal remains), **forensic entomologist** (insects), **DNA analyst**, **firearms examiner**, **forensic nurse** (care and evidence collection for victims) and **forensic psychologist or psychiatrist**.',
'Education varies: technicians often need a bachelor’s degree in a natural science; pathologists complete medical school and residency; anthropologists and psychologists typically earn graduate degrees. Attention to detail, objectivity, ethics and clear communication are essential.',
'Many forensic professionals testify in court as **expert witnesses**.',
match('Career and focus',[['Forensic pathologist','Autopsies and cause of death'],['Forensic odontologist','Teeth and dental identification'],['Forensic anthropologist','Skeletal remains'],['Forensic entomologist','Insects and time since death'],['Forensic toxicologist','Drugs and poisons'],['Forensic nurse','Care and evidence collection for patients']])]],
['Ethics and scientific integrity',[
'Forensic scientists must be **objective**, follow validated methods and report findings honestly, even when they do not support a particular theory. Reviews of the field have emphasized the importance of **validated techniques**, **error rates**, **standards** and avoiding **cognitive bias**—for example, by limiting exposure to unnecessary case information.',
'Maintaining **chain of custody**, documenting every step and acknowledging uncertainty are ethical obligations, because conclusions can affect people’s lives and liberty.',
'In your written conclusion, base every statement on evidence and avoid overstating certainty.',
chk('Which practice best reflects forensic scientific integrity?',['Reporting findings honestly and acknowledging uncertainty','Choosing results that fit a suspect','Skipping documentation to save time','Guessing without evidence'],'Objectivity and documentation protect justice.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Planning the year',{a:{text:'Your two-person team is preparing for Forensic Science. (Scenario invented for practice.) Which statement about scoring is correct?',choices:[['The team test average is added to the case study score','b','Correct.',true],['Only the case study counts','b','Both rounds count.',false]]},b:{text:'In Round Two, what must your conclusion include?',choices:[['Time of death range, immediate cause, manner, contributing conditions and supporting evidence','c','Correct.',true],['Only the suspect’s name','c','The rubric lists five content sections.',false]]},c:{text:'You study the two official references for the test and review anatomy and disease for the case study.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Two-member team; 50-question test (team average qualifies, is added to the final score and breaks ties); 40-minute secret case analysis plus 5 minutes to upload a PDF; battery-powered device, no internet research.','Conclusion (80 points): time of death range, immediate cause, manner (natural, accident, suicide, homicide, undetermined), contributing conditions and evidence, scored with knowledge application, clarity and mechanics.','History: Locard (exchange principle), Orfila (toxicology), Landsteiner (ABO), Bertillon, Goddard, Jeffreys (DNA); medical examiners are usually physicians, coroners often elected; careers include pathologist, toxicologist, odontologist, anthropologist, entomologist and DNA analyst.'],terms:[['Forensic science','Application of science to legal questions.'],['Medical examiner','A physician appointed to investigate deaths.'],['Locard’s exchange principle','Every contact leaves a trace.']],mcqs:[q('How many members are on a Forensic Science team?','2','3','4','6','Pairs.'),q('How long do teams have to analyze the case and write the conclusion?','40 minutes','15 minutes','60 minutes','2 hours','Plus 5 to upload.'),q('Who stated the exchange principle?','Edmond Locard','Alec Jeffreys','Karl Landsteiner','Calvin Goddard','Every contact leaves a trace.'),q('Who is called the father of toxicology?','Mathieu Orfila','Francis Galton','Alphonse Bertillon','Edward Henry','Poisons.'),q('Who developed DNA fingerprinting?','Alec Jeffreys','Karl Landsteiner','Edmond Locard','Mathieu Orfila','Mid-1980s.'),q('A coroner is often:','An elected official','Always a physician','A forensic entomologist','A judge','Varies by jurisdiction.'),q('Which is one of the five manners of death?','Undetermined','Cardiac','Trauma','Infection','Natural, accident, suicide, homicide, undetermined.'),q('Which test area is weighted 20%?','Forensic Toxicology and Pharmacology','Forensic Entomology','DNA Analysis','Forensic Anthropology','Also crime scene and death investigation.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe the Forensic Science event and explain the roles of medical examiners and forensic specialists in determining the cause and manner of death.',rubric:[['Event',['two','test','team average','case study','40 minutes','pdf','conclusion']],['Conclusion elements',['time of death','cause','manner','contributing','evidence']],['Death investigators',['medical examiner','physician','coroner','elected','forensic pathologist','autopsy']],['Specialists',['toxicologist','anthropologist','entomologist','odontologist','dna','objectivity']]],model:'Forensic Science is a two-member team event for students with a background in medical terminology and anatomy and physiology. In Round One, both members take a 50-question test on forensic history, crime scene and death investigation, toxicology, anthropology, entomology, blood and biological evidence, DNA analysis and forensic psychology, and the team average both qualifies the team and counts toward the final score. In Round Two, teams receive the same secret case study, which may include a scene, police report and autopsy findings, and have 40 minutes to write a conclusion stating the time of death range, immediate cause of death, manner of death, contributing conditions and the evidence supporting each, then five minutes to upload it as a PDF. In real investigations, sudden, unexpected or violent deaths are investigated by a medical examiner—usually a physician such as a forensic pathologist—or a coroner, who in many places is an elected official and may hire pathologists to perform autopsies. They determine the cause of death, the injury or disease that led to death, and the manner of death, classified as natural, accident, suicide, homicide or undetermined. They rely on specialists: toxicologists identify drugs and poisons, forensic anthropologists analyze skeletal remains, forensic entomologists use insect evidence to estimate time since death, odontologists identify people from teeth and DNA analysts link biological evidence to individuals. All must work objectively, document carefully and acknowledge uncertainty.'},sources:[RULES,NIJ,BLS_FS]};

export const FS2={title:'Crime scene investigation and evidence',hook:'The first minutes at a scene can make or break an investigation. A single footprint walked over or an unlabeled bag of evidence can lose information forever. Crime scene and death investigation is 20% of the test, and understanding how evidence is found, documented and preserved helps you interpret the case in Round Two.',objectives:['Describe the steps of crime scene processing.','Compare crime scene search patterns.','Classify types of physical evidence.','Explain chain of custody and evidence packaging.'],sections:[
['Securing and documenting the scene',[
'The first responding officer’s priorities are **safety**, **aid to anyone injured** and **securing the scene** to prevent contamination—setting up a perimeter, controlling entry and keeping a **log** of everyone who enters. Investigators then conduct a **walk-through** to plan their approach.',
'Documentation uses three methods: **notes** (detailed, timed observations), **photographs** (overall, mid-range and close-up, with and without scales) and **sketches** (rough sketches at the scene with measurements, later finalized). Video may also be used. Documentation must happen **before** evidence is moved.',
'In case studies, scene descriptions and photos provide clues about position, surroundings and possible sequence of events.',
"Investigators also note conditions that may change quickly, such as weather, lighting, temperature, smells and the position of doors and windows. These details can be critical later and may not be recoverable once the scene is disturbed.",
order('Processing a scene',['Ensure safety and aid victims','Secure the scene and start an entry log','Conduct an initial walk-through','Document with notes, photos and sketches','Search for and collect evidence','Package, label and maintain chain of custody'])]],
['Search patterns',[
'Investigators choose a **search pattern** suited to the scene. **Line (strip)** searches use people walking in parallel lines—good for large outdoor areas. **Grid** searches repeat a line search at right angles for thoroughness. **Spiral** searches move inward or outward in a circle—useful for small areas with one searcher. **Zone (quadrant)** searches divide the area into sections, useful indoors. **Wheel (ray)** searches move outward from a center along spokes.',
'Searches should be **systematic** so no area is missed, and evidence locations should be documented before collection.',
'Test questions frequently ask which pattern fits a given scene.',
"Indoors, investigators often begin at the point of entry and work systematically toward the body, searching floors, walls, ceilings and furniture. Outdoors, they consider paths to and from the scene, discarded items and impressions in soil or snow.",
match('Search pattern and best use',[['Line (strip)','Large outdoor areas with several searchers'],['Grid','Thorough double coverage'],['Spiral','Small area with one searcher'],['Zone (quadrant)','Rooms or vehicles divided into sections'],['Wheel (ray)','Circular area searched along spokes'],['Systematic searching','Ensures no area is missed']])]],
['Types of evidence',[
'**Physical evidence** includes objects such as weapons, fibers, glass and fingerprints. **Biological evidence** includes blood, saliva, hair and other body fluids. **Trace evidence** consists of small transferable materials such as fibers, hair, soil, paint and glass fragments. **Impression evidence** includes fingerprints, footwear and tire marks and tool marks. **Testimonial evidence** comes from witnesses.',
'Evidence can be **direct** (proves a fact, such as an eyewitness) or **circumstantial** (implies a fact, such as fingerprints at a scene). **Class characteristics** narrow evidence to a group (a shoe size and brand), while **individual characteristics** can link evidence to a single source (unique wear patterns, DNA, fingerprints).',
'Locard’s exchange principle explains why trace evidence is valuable: contact transfers material between people, objects and places.',
"Trace evidence is analyzed with microscopes and instruments that compare color, shape, chemical composition and other properties. Even when it cannot individualize, trace evidence can corroborate or contradict accounts of where a person has been.",
sort('Class or individual characteristic?',['Class characteristic','Individual characteristic'],[['Blood type A',0],['A DNA profile',1],['Shoe size and brand',0],['Unique cuts on a shoe sole',1],['Fiber type (nylon)',0],['A fingerprint',1]])]],
['Fingerprints and impression evidence',[
'Fingerprints are formed before birth and remain unchanged throughout life, making them useful for identification. The three basic patterns are **loops** (most common), **whorls** and **arches** (least common). Prints may be **patent** (visible, such as in blood or paint), **plastic** (molded into soft material like wax) or **latent** (invisible until developed with powders, chemicals such as ninhydrin, or alternative light sources).',
'Fingerprints are compared using ridge characteristics (**minutiae**) such as bifurcations, ridge endings and dots, and searched in databases such as the FBI’s automated system. Footwear, tire and tool marks are compared for class and individual characteristics.',
'In case studies, fingerprints may help identify a person or show who handled an object.',
flip('Fingerprint basics',[['Loop','Most common pattern'],['Whorl','Circular or spiral pattern'],['Arch','Least common pattern'],['Latent print','Invisible until developed'],['Patent print','Visible print'],['Plastic print','Impression in soft material']])]],
['Collecting and packaging evidence',[
'Wear **gloves** and other protection, change gloves between items and use clean tools to prevent contamination. Collect the **least stable** evidence first (for example, items that may be lost to weather). Package items **separately**. **Biological evidence** should be dried and packaged in **paper** (breathable) containers, because plastic traps moisture and promotes mold and degradation. Liquids and volatile materials may need airtight containers.',
'Each package is **sealed** and **labeled** with the case number, item description, location, date, time and collector’s initials. **Control samples** (known samples from the scene) help comparison.',
'Proper packaging preserves evidence for laboratory testing and court.',
sort('Correct packaging?',['Correct','Incorrect'],[['Dried bloody shirt in a paper bag',0],['Wet bloody shirt sealed in plastic',1],['Each item packaged separately',0],['Several items mixed in one bag',1],['Sealed and labeled with initials and date',0]])]],
['Chain of custody',[
'**Chain of custody** is the documented, unbroken record of everyone who handled a piece of evidence, when and why, from collection to court. Each transfer is signed and dated. A broken chain can make evidence **inadmissible** because it raises doubts about tampering or contamination.',
'Chain of custody applies to all evidence, including specimens from autopsies such as blood and tissue for toxicology.',
'In your written conclusion, you can trust evidence presented in the case as properly documented unless the case suggests otherwise.',
"Laboratories that analyze evidence follow quality standards, and many are accredited, meaning outside reviewers confirm they use validated methods and proper documentation.",
chk('What is chain of custody?',['A documented record of everyone who handled evidence','The order of crime scene photos','A type of search pattern','A fingerprint pattern'],'An unbroken chain protects admissibility.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Outdoor scene',{a:{text:'A large wooded area must be searched with six investigators. (Scenario invented for practice.) Which pattern fits?',choices:[['Line (strip) search','b','Correct.',true],['Spiral search with one person','b','Better for small areas.',false]]},b:{text:'A damp shirt with stains is found. How is it packaged?',choices:[['Air-dried, then placed in a paper bag, sealed and labeled','c','Correct.',true],['Sealed wet in plastic','c','Moisture promotes degradation.',false]]},c:{text:'Each transfer of the shirt is recorded on the chain of custody form.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Secure the scene, log entries, walk through and document with notes, photos (overall, mid-range, close-up with scales) and sketches before moving evidence.','Search patterns: line for large outdoor areas, grid for thoroughness, spiral for small areas, zone for rooms, wheel for circular areas; evidence types include physical, biological, trace, impression and testimonial; class vs individual characteristics.','Fingerprints: loops (most common), whorls, arches; latent, patent, plastic; package items separately, biological evidence dried in paper; maintain an unbroken chain of custody.'],terms:[['Chain of custody','A documented record of evidence handling from collection to court.'],['Trace evidence','Small transferable materials such as fibers, hair and soil.'],['Latent print','An invisible fingerprint that must be developed.']],mcqs:[q('Which search pattern suits a small area with one searcher?','Spiral','Line','Grid','Zone','Circular.'),q('Which is the most common fingerprint pattern?','Loop','Whorl','Arch','Composite','About 60–65%.'),q('Biological evidence should be packaged in:','Paper after drying','Sealed plastic while wet','Glass jars always','Aluminum foil','Prevents mold.'),q('A fingerprint in wax is a:','Plastic print','Latent print','Patent print','Ridge count','Molded impression.'),q('Which is an individual characteristic?','A DNA profile','Blood type A','Shoe size','Fiber type','Unique source.'),q('What makes evidence inadmissible if broken?','Chain of custody','Search pattern','Sketch scale','Fingerprint class','Documentation.'),q('Small fibers transferred between people are:','Trace evidence','Testimonial evidence','Direct evidence','Impression evidence','Locard.'),q('What is documented before evidence is moved?','Its location with notes, photos and sketches','Nothing','Only the suspect','Only the weather','Documentation first.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how investigators process a crime scene from arrival to evidence submission, including documentation, search patterns, evidence types and chain of custody.',rubric:[['Securing',['safety','secure','perimeter','log','walk-through']],['Documentation',['notes','photographs','sketches','scale','before moving']],['Search and evidence',['pattern','line','grid','spiral','zone','trace','biological','impression','class','individual']],['Collection and custody',['gloves','separately','paper','label','seal','chain of custody']]],model:'When investigators arrive, the first priorities are safety and helping anyone injured. Officers then secure the scene with a perimeter, control entry and keep a log of everyone who enters to prevent contamination. The lead investigator conducts a walk-through to plan the search. Before anything is moved, the scene is documented with detailed, timed notes; photographs taken as overall, mid-range and close-up shots, with and without scales; and rough sketches with measurements that are later finalized. Investigators choose a systematic search pattern suited to the scene—a line search for a large outdoor area with many searchers, a grid for thorough double coverage, a spiral for a small area, zones for rooms or vehicles or a wheel pattern for circular areas. They look for physical, biological, trace, impression and testimonial evidence, noting class characteristics that narrow evidence to a group and individual characteristics, such as DNA or fingerprints, that can link it to one source. Collectors wear gloves, change them between items and package each item separately; biological evidence is dried and placed in paper containers to prevent degradation, while volatile liquids go in airtight containers. Every package is sealed and labeled with the case number, description, location, date, time and collector’s initials, and every transfer is recorded in an unbroken chain of custody so the evidence remains admissible in court.'},sources:[RULES,NIJ,NIST]};

export const FS3={title:'Death investigation and time of death',hook:'Determining when someone died narrows the list of possible events and people involved. The body begins changing immediately after death in predictable ways—cooling, stiffening and discoloring—and the stomach contents, eye fluid and environment all add clues. In Round Two, the time of death range is worth 10 points, and these concepts are central to the test.',objectives:['Distinguish cause, mechanism and manner of death.','Describe the autopsy process.','Explain algor, livor and rigor mortis and decomposition.','Estimate a time of death range from multiple indicators.'],sections:[
['Cause, mechanism and manner',[
'The **cause of death** is the injury or disease that started the chain of events leading to death (for example, a gunshot wound to the chest or coronary artery disease). The **mechanism of death** is the physiological change that resulted (for example, blood loss or cardiac arrhythmia). The **manner of death** classifies how the death came about: **natural**, **accident**, **suicide**, **homicide** or **undetermined**.',
'The same mechanism can arise from different causes and manners. Blood loss could result from an accidental fall onto glass, a self-inflicted wound or an assault. That is why investigators combine **autopsy findings** with **scene evidence** and **history**.',
'In your written conclusion, the immediate cause is the medical cause; the manner is one of the five categories; contributing conditions are factors such as heart disease or intoxication that contributed but did not directly cause death.',
sort('Cause, mechanism or manner?',['Cause','Mechanism','Manner'],[['Coronary artery disease',0],['Cardiac arrhythmia',1],['Natural',2],['Blunt force injury to the head',0],['Brain swelling',1],['Accident',2]])]],
['The autopsy',[
'An **autopsy** is a systematic examination of a body after death. A **forensic autopsy** begins with identification and an **external examination**—clothing, height, weight, injuries, marks, postmortem changes and evidence such as trace materials or gunshot residue. The **internal examination** inspects and weighs organs and looks for injuries and disease.',
'Specimens are collected for **toxicology** (blood, urine, vitreous humor, stomach contents, liver) and **histology** (tissue under a microscope). The pathologist combines findings with scene information to determine cause and manner of death.',
'Autopsy reports in case studies list key findings; read them carefully for clues such as organ weights, injuries and toxicology results.',
order('Forensic autopsy steps',['Identification and photographs','External examination','Collection of trace evidence','Internal examination of organs','Collection of toxicology and tissue specimens','Correlation with scene and history'])]],
['Algor mortis',[
'**Algor mortis** is the cooling of the body after death until it reaches the temperature of its surroundings. A traditional estimate is that the body cools about **1.5 °F per hour** under typical conditions. A common teaching formula is: **hours since death ≈ (98.6 °F − body temperature) ÷ 1.5**.',
'Cooling is affected by **environment** (temperature, wind, water), **clothing**, **body size and fat**, and **fever or exertion** before death. Bodies in water cool faster; obese or clothed bodies cool more slowly. Because of these variables, algor mortis provides an estimate, not an exact time.',
ex('Estimating time from body temperature','An invented case: body temperature is 89.6 °F in a room at 70 °F.',['Temperature drop = 98.6 − 89.6 = 9.0 °F','Hours ≈ 9.0 ÷ 1.5 = 6 hours','The body has not yet reached room temperature','Combine with rigor and livor for a range'],'About 6 hours since death, as one part of a time range (invented for practice).')]],
['Livor mortis and rigor mortis',[
'**Livor mortis** (lividity) is the purplish discoloration caused by blood settling in the lowest parts of the body due to gravity. It typically appears within about **30 minutes to 2 hours**, becomes noticeable over several hours and becomes **fixed** at roughly **8–12 hours**, after which it no longer shifts when the body is moved. Lividity in areas that are not the lowest parts suggests the body was **moved**. Cherry-red lividity can suggest **carbon monoxide** poisoning.',
'**Rigor mortis** is stiffening of the muscles caused by chemical changes as ATP is depleted. It usually begins in about **2–6 hours**, often starting in small muscles of the face and jaw, becomes **full** at about **12 hours**, persists for some hours and then **disappears** over roughly **24–36 hours** as decomposition begins. Heat speeds rigor; cold slows it.',
'These ranges vary with conditions, so combine them with other evidence.',
flip('Postmortem changes',[['Algor mortis','Body cooling, about 1.5 °F per hour'],['Livor mortis','Blood settling; fixed at about 8–12 hours'],['Rigor mortis','Stiffening; begins about 2–6 hours'],['Full rigor','About 12 hours'],['Rigor disappears','About 24–36 hours'],['Cherry-red lividity','May suggest carbon monoxide']])]],
['Other time indicators',[
'**Stomach contents**: a meal usually leaves the stomach within about **4–6 hours** (light meals faster, heavy meals slower). Undigested food suggests death occurred soon after eating; an empty stomach suggests several hours had passed. **Vitreous humor potassium** (fluid in the eye) rises steadily after death and can help estimate time. **Corneal clouding** develops within hours.',
'**Decomposition** follows stages—fresh, bloat, active decay, advanced decay and skeletal—at rates affected by temperature, moisture and insects. **Forensic entomology** becomes the main tool once a body has been dead for more than a few days. **Scene clues** such as mail, newspapers, receipts, phone records and witness reports also narrow the range.',
'Strong conclusions combine several indicators into a **range** consistent with all the evidence.',
match('Indicator and what it suggests',[['Undigested meal in stomach','Death soon after eating'],['Empty stomach','Several hours after the last meal'],['Rising vitreous potassium','Time since death increasing'],['Corneal clouding','Hours have passed'],['Bloating','Decomposition underway'],['Uncollected mail','Last time the person was active']])]],
['Building a time of death range',[
'Investigators rarely state an exact minute. Instead they give a **range** consistent with the evidence: **body temperature**, **rigor**, **livor**, **stomach contents**, **entomology** and **scene and witness information**. Start with the **last known alive** time and the **time found**, then narrow the window using each indicator.',
'In Round Two, the rubric awards full credit for the correct date and approximate time and partial credit for a close approximation. State the range clearly (for example, “between 10:00 p.m. on March 3 and 1:00 a.m. on March 4”) and list the evidence supporting it.',
'Note conditions that could shift estimates, such as a cold room or heavy clothing.',
order('Estimating a range',['Note last known alive and time found','Estimate from body temperature','Check rigor and livor stages','Use stomach contents and other indicators','Adjust for environment and clothing','State a range with supporting evidence'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Indoor case',{a:{text:'An invented case: a body at 92.6 °F in a 72 °F room, full rigor not yet present, lividity still shifting, partially digested meal in the stomach. (Scenario invented for practice.) Roughly how long since death by temperature?',choices:[['About 4 hours','b','Correct. (98.6 − 92.6) ÷ 1.5 = 4.',true],['About 24 hours','b','Too long for this temperature.',false]]},b:{text:'Do the other findings agree?',choices:[['Yes—rigor developing, livor not fixed and a partly digested meal fit a few hours','c','Correct.',true],['No—they suggest several days','c','Several days would show fixed livor and decomposition.',false]]},c:{text:'You report a range of roughly 3–5 hours before discovery, citing each indicator.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Cause is the injury or disease, mechanism is the physiological change and manner is natural, accident, suicide, homicide or undetermined; contributing conditions add to but do not directly cause death.','Forensic autopsies include identification, external and internal examination and specimens for toxicology and histology, correlated with scene evidence.','Algor mortis about 1.5 °F per hour (hours ≈ [98.6 − temp] ÷ 1.5); livor appears within hours and fixes at about 8–12 hours; rigor begins about 2–6 hours, is full at about 12 and disappears by about 24–36; combine with stomach contents, vitreous potassium, decomposition and scene clues to state a range.'],terms:[['Algor mortis','Postmortem cooling of the body.'],['Livor mortis','Settling of blood causing purplish discoloration.'],['Rigor mortis','Postmortem stiffening of muscles.']],mcqs:[q('Using the teaching formula, a body at 92.6 °F has been dead about:','4 hours','1 hour','12 hours','24 hours','(98.6 − 92.6) ÷ 1.5.'),q('Livor mortis becomes fixed at about:','8–12 hours','30 minutes','3 days','1 week','No longer shifts.'),q('Rigor mortis usually disappears after about:','24–36 hours','2 hours','1 week','10 minutes','Decomposition begins.'),q('Which is a manner of death?','Homicide','Arrhythmia','Blood loss','Coronary artery disease','Five categories.'),q('Lividity in areas that are not the lowest parts suggests:','The body was moved','Death was natural','Carbon monoxide','Drowning','Fixed lividity.'),q('Which fluid is used to measure postmortem potassium?','Vitreous humor','Saliva','Sweat','Bile','Eye fluid.'),q('Cherry-red lividity may suggest:','Carbon monoxide poisoning','Drowning','Hanging','Hypothermia only','Carboxyhemoglobin.'),q('A meal usually leaves the stomach within about:','4–6 hours','30 minutes','2 days','1 week','Varies with meal.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how investigators estimate a time of death range using postmortem changes and other indicators, including a sample calculation with invented data.',rubric:[['Algor mortis',['algor','cooling','1.5','98.6','calculation','environment']],['Livor and rigor',['livor','fixed','8-12','rigor','2-6','12','24-36']],['Other indicators',['stomach','vitreous','potassium','decomposition','entomology','scene']],['Range',['range','last known','found','combine','consistent','evidence']]],model:'Investigators estimate a range rather than an exact time by combining several indicators with the times the person was last known alive and was found. Algor mortis, the cooling of the body, is often estimated at about 1.5 °F per hour; for example, in an invented case a body measuring 89.6 °F gives (98.6 − 89.6) ÷ 1.5 = 6 hours, adjusted for room temperature, clothing and body size. Livor mortis, the settling of blood that causes purplish discoloration, appears within about half an hour to two hours and becomes fixed around 8–12 hours; if lividity still shifts when the body is moved, death was likely less than about 8–12 hours earlier, and lividity in unexpected places suggests the body was moved. Rigor mortis, the stiffening of muscles, usually begins in about 2–6 hours, is full at about 12 hours and disappears over about 24–36 hours, so developing rigor without full stiffness fits a death within the past several hours. Stomach contents add information because meals usually leave the stomach within 4–6 hours, and rising potassium in the vitreous humor, corneal clouding, decomposition stages and insect evidence help for longer intervals. Scene clues such as receipts, phone use and uncollected mail also narrow the window. In the invented case, a body at 89.6 °F with developing rigor, unfixed lividity and a partially digested meal points to death roughly 5–7 hours before discovery, and I would state that range with each supporting fact.'},sources:[RULES,NIJ,NIST]};

export const FS4={title:'Injuries and causes of death',hook:'An autopsy report may describe an abrasion on the forehead, petechiae in the eyes or froth in the airways. Each finding points toward particular causes and mechanisms of death. Knowing how injuries and conditions are classified—and what they suggest about how death occurred—is essential for the immediate cause and manner sections of your Round Two conclusion.',objectives:['Classify blunt force and sharp force injuries.','Describe general features of gunshot wounds and range of fire.','Recognize findings associated with asphyxia, drowning and other mechanisms.','Distinguish natural causes and contributing conditions.'],sections:[
['Blunt force injuries',[
'**Blunt force** injuries result from impact with a blunt object or surface. An **abrasion** is a scrape removing the outer skin layer; its direction can show how force was applied. A **contusion** (bruise) is bleeding under the skin from broken blood vessels; its color changes over time but aging bruises by color is unreliable. A **laceration** is a tear in the skin with irregular edges and **tissue bridging** (strands of tissue connecting the sides).',
'Internal blunt injuries can include fractures and organ damage. Falls, motor vehicle collisions and assaults commonly cause blunt force injuries. The pattern and location of injuries help distinguish an accidental fall from an assault—for example, injuries on several sides of the head are less consistent with a single fall.',
'In case studies, note the type, location and number of injuries and whether they match the reported events.',
"Pattern injuries, which reproduce the shape of the object that caused them—such as a belt buckle or the sole of a shoe—can link an injury to a specific type of object found at the scene.",
match('Injury and description',[['Abrasion','Scrape removing the outer skin layer'],['Contusion','Bruise from bleeding under the skin'],['Laceration','Tear with irregular edges and tissue bridging'],['Fracture','Broken bone'],['Pattern injury','Injury shaped like the object that caused it'],['Defensive injury','Injury on hands or arms from protecting oneself']])]],
['Sharp force injuries',[
'**Sharp force** injuries come from edged or pointed objects. An **incised wound** (cut) is longer than it is deep, with clean edges and no tissue bridging. A **stab wound** is deeper than it is long. **Chop wounds** come from heavy edged objects and combine cutting and crushing.',
'**Defensive wounds** on the hands and forearms suggest a struggle. **Hesitation marks**—multiple shallow, parallel cuts—are often associated with self-inflicted wounds. These patterns help determine manner of death, but investigators always consider the full context.',
'Distinguishing incised from lacerated wounds (clean edges versus tissue bridging) is a commonly tested concept.',
sort('Incised wound or laceration?',['Incised wound','Laceration'],[['Clean, straight edges',0],['Tissue bridging between edges',1],['Caused by a sharp blade',0],['Caused by blunt impact',1],['Longer than it is deep',0],['Irregular, torn edges',1]])]],
['Gunshot wounds',[
'Gunshot wounds are described as **entrance** or **exit** wounds. Entrance wounds are usually smaller and round with an **abrasion ring**; exit wounds are often larger and irregular, without an abrasion ring. **Range of fire** is estimated from features around the entrance: **contact** wounds may show soot and a muzzle imprint; **close-range** wounds show **stippling** (tattooing) from unburned powder; **distant** wounds lack soot and stippling.',
'**Gunshot residue** on hands or clothing can indicate firing or proximity to a firearm. Firearms examiners compare marks on bullets and cartridge cases. Range and wound location help determine manner of death, along with scene evidence.',
'Questions often ask what stippling indicates (close range) or which features distinguish entrance from exit wounds.',
flip('Gunshot wound features',[['Entrance wound','Smaller, round, abrasion ring'],['Exit wound','Larger, irregular, no abrasion ring'],['Contact range','Soot and possible muzzle imprint'],['Close range','Stippling from unburned powder'],['Distant range','No soot or stippling'],['Gunshot residue','Particles from a fired weapon']])]],
['Asphyxia',[
'**Asphyxia** is a lack of oxygen to the tissues. Types include **suffocation** (blocked airway or lack of oxygen in the environment), **choking** (obstruction inside the airway), **strangulation** (pressure on the neck by a ligature or hands), **hanging** (pressure on the neck from body weight) and **positional** or **compression** asphyxia (body position or chest compression prevents breathing).',
'Common findings include **petechial hemorrhages** (tiny pinpoint bleeds, often in the eyes and face) and **cyanosis**. Neck injuries such as ligature marks and fractures of the hyoid bone or larynx structures may help distinguish types. Interpretation requires caution because some findings occur in other conditions.',
'Scene evidence, such as a ligature or position of the body, is essential to determine manner.',
match('Asphyxia type and description',[['Suffocation','Blocked airway or lack of oxygen'],['Choking','Obstruction inside the airway'],['Strangulation','Neck pressure by ligature or hands'],['Hanging','Neck pressure from body weight'],['Compression asphyxia','Chest compression prevents breathing'],['Petechiae','Pinpoint hemorrhages often in the eyes']])]],
['Drowning, temperature and other mechanisms',[
'**Drowning** is death from submersion in liquid. Findings may include **froth** in the airways, water in the lungs and stomach and diatoms (microscopic algae) in tissues. In **freshwater** drowning, water is absorbed into the blood, which can cause hemolysis (destruction of red blood cells); in **saltwater**, fluid shifts into the lungs. Bodies recovered from water also show changes such as wrinkled skin of the hands and feet.',
'Other mechanisms include **hypothermia** (dangerously low body temperature), **hyperthermia** (heat stroke), **electrocution** (entry and exit burns may be small) and **burns and smoke inhalation** (soot in the airways and elevated carboxyhemoglobin show the person was breathing during a fire).',
'Each finding should be connected to physiology in your written explanation.',
sort('Finding and likely mechanism',['Drowning','Fire and smoke inhalation','Electrocution'],[['Froth in airways after submersion',0],['Soot in the airways',1],['Small entry and exit burns',2],['Elevated carboxyhemoglobin',1],['Diatoms in tissues',0]])]],
['Natural causes and contributing conditions',[
'Many deaths investigated by medical examiners are **natural**. Common natural causes of sudden death include **coronary artery disease** and heart attack, **cardiac arrhythmias**, **stroke**, **pulmonary embolism**, **ruptured aneurysm**, severe **asthma** and **diabetic complications**. Autopsy findings such as narrowed coronary arteries or a blood clot in the lungs support these causes.',
'**Contributing conditions** are diseases or circumstances that contributed to death but did not directly cause it, such as hypertension, obesity, chronic lung disease or intoxication. For example, a person with severe heart disease who dies during a stressful assault may have a homicide manner with heart disease as a contributing condition.',
'Your conclusion should separate the **immediate cause** from **contributing conditions** clearly.',
chk('A person with severe coronary artery disease dies after a minor fall. The fall did not cause serious injury. What is most likely?',['A natural death from heart disease, with the fall noted in the circumstances','A homicide','A suicide','Undetermined by default'],'Consider all evidence; natural disease is common.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Reading autopsy findings',{a:{text:'An invented autopsy notes a round wound with an abrasion ring and stippling on the chest, plus a larger irregular wound on the back. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do these suggest?',choices:[['A close-range gunshot entering the chest and exiting the back','b','Correct.',true],['A distant shot entering the back','b','The abrasion ring and stippling mark the chest entrance at close range.',false]]},b:{text:'Toxicology shows a high blood alcohol level. How is it listed?',choices:[['As a contributing condition, not the immediate cause','c','Correct.',true],['As the manner of death','c','Manner is one of the five categories.',false]]},c:{text:'You list the gunshot wound as the immediate cause and support the manner with scene evidence.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Blunt force: abrasion, contusion, laceration (irregular edges, tissue bridging); sharp force: incised (longer than deep, clean edges), stab (deeper than long); defensive wounds and hesitation marks help interpret manner.','Gunshot entrances are smaller with an abrasion ring; exits larger and irregular; contact wounds show soot, close range shows stippling, distant shows neither.','Asphyxia types include suffocation, choking, strangulation, hanging and compression, often with petechiae; drowning, hypothermia, electrocution and smoke inhalation have characteristic findings; separate the immediate cause from contributing conditions such as heart disease or intoxication.'],terms:[['Laceration','A tear in the skin with irregular edges caused by blunt force.'],['Stippling','Marks from unburned gunpowder indicating a close-range shot.'],['Petechiae','Pinpoint hemorrhages associated with some types of asphyxia.']],mcqs:[q('Tissue bridging is characteristic of a:','Laceration','Incised wound','Stab wound','Gunshot exit wound','Blunt force tear.'),q('A wound deeper than it is long is a:','Stab wound','Incised wound','Abrasion','Contusion','Sharp force.'),q('Stippling around a gunshot wound indicates:','Close-range fire','Distant fire','An exit wound','No firearm involved','Unburned powder.'),q('An entrance gunshot wound usually has:','An abrasion ring','Irregular, everted edges and no ring','No hole','Hesitation marks','Smaller and round.'),q('Pinpoint hemorrhages in the eyes are called:','Petechiae','Lividity','Rigor','Stippling','Asphyxia finding.'),q('Soot in the airways of a fire victim indicates:','The person was breathing during the fire','Death before the fire','Drowning','Electrocution','Inhalation.'),q('Hesitation marks are often associated with:','Self-inflicted wounds','Gunshot wounds','Natural death','Drowning','Shallow parallel cuts.'),q('Which is a common natural cause of sudden death?','Coronary artery disease','Strangulation','Stab wound','Gunshot wound','Natural manner.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how injury findings help determine the immediate cause and manner of death, using examples of blunt force, sharp force, gunshot and asphyxia findings.',rubric:[['Blunt and sharp force',['abrasion','contusion','laceration','tissue bridging','incised','stab','defensive','hesitation']],['Gunshot',['entrance','exit','abrasion ring','soot','stippling','range']],['Asphyxia and other',['asphyxia','petechiae','strangulation','hanging','drowning','smoke']],['Cause vs manner',['immediate cause','manner','contributing','scene','context']]],model:'Injury findings identify the immediate cause of death and, combined with scene evidence, help determine manner. Blunt force injuries include abrasions, contusions and lacerations—tears with irregular edges and tissue bridging—and their number and location matter: several injuries on different sides of the head are less consistent with a single accidental fall than with an assault. Sharp force injuries include incised wounds, which are longer than deep with clean edges, and stab wounds, which are deeper than long; defensive wounds on the hands suggest a struggle, while multiple shallow, parallel hesitation marks are often associated with self-inflicted injuries. Gunshot wounds are interpreted by entrance features—a smaller, round wound with an abrasion ring—and exit features—a larger, irregular wound—while soot suggests contact range, stippling suggests close range and the absence of both suggests distant fire; a distant wound in a location a person could not reach would argue against suicide. Asphyxia findings such as petechial hemorrhages, ligature marks and neck injuries point to strangulation or hanging, and scene evidence such as the ligature and body position helps distinguish them. Throughout, the immediate cause is the medical cause, such as a gunshot wound to the chest, while factors like heart disease or intoxication are listed as contributing conditions, and the manner—natural, accident, suicide, homicide or undetermined—is determined only after considering all of the evidence together.'},sources:[RULES,NIST,NIJ]};

export const FS5={title:'Forensic toxicology and pharmacology',hook:'Some causes of death leave no visible injury at all. An overdose, a poisoning or a dangerous combination of medications may be detectable only in the laboratory. Forensic toxicology and pharmacology make up 20% of the test, and toxicology reports are often the key to cause, manner and contributing conditions in case studies.',objectives:['Describe the role of forensic toxicology and the specimens used.','Distinguish screening and confirmatory tests.','Describe major drug classes and their effects.','Interpret alcohol, carbon monoxide and other common toxicology findings.'],sections:[
['What forensic toxicologists do',[
'**Forensic toxicology** detects and interprets **drugs, alcohol and poisons** in biological specimens to determine whether they caused or contributed to death, impairment or illness. Toxicologists support death investigations, impaired-driving cases and workplace or sports testing.',
'Key principles: “**The dose makes the poison**”—almost any substance can be toxic at a high enough dose—and individual factors such as **tolerance**, **age**, **body size**, **disease** and **drug interactions** affect toxicity. Toxicologists interpret concentrations in context rather than relying on a single number.',
'In case studies, a toxicology report may reveal an overdose (cause), intoxication (contributing condition) or the absence of drugs (helping rule out a manner).',
chk('What does “the dose makes the poison” mean?',['Toxicity depends on the amount of a substance','Only illegal drugs are poisons','All poisons are equally dangerous','Small doses are always fatal'],'A principle attributed to Paracelsus.')]],
['Specimens and postmortem considerations',[
'Common specimens include **blood** (often from the femoral vein, which is less affected by postmortem changes than heart blood), **urine** (shows past exposure), **vitreous humor** (protected and useful for alcohol and electrolytes), **stomach contents** (recent ingestion), **liver** (drug metabolism) and **hair** (long-term exposure over weeks to months).',
'**Postmortem redistribution** is the movement of drugs from tissues into blood after death, which can raise measured concentrations in some sites. Decomposition can also produce alcohol. That is why specimen site and condition matter, and why toxicologists may compare samples.',
'Chain of custody applies to all toxicology specimens.',
"Toxicologists also consider the timing of drug use. Parent drugs and their metabolites can indicate whether a substance was taken recently or hours earlier; for example, finding only metabolites may suggest use some time before death.",
match('Specimen and what it shows',[['Femoral blood','Concentration at time of death, less redistribution'],['Urine','Past exposure'],['Vitreous humor','Alcohol and electrolytes, protected site'],['Stomach contents','Recent ingestion'],['Liver','Drug metabolism'],['Hair','Long-term exposure']])]],
['Screening and confirmatory testing',[
'Testing usually occurs in two stages. **Screening tests**, such as **immunoassays**, quickly detect classes of drugs but can produce **false positives**. **Confirmatory tests**, such as **gas chromatography–mass spectrometry (GC-MS)** or liquid chromatography–mass spectrometry, identify specific substances and measure concentrations with high accuracy.',
'Chromatography separates components of a mixture; mass spectrometry identifies them by their mass patterns. GC-MS is often called the “gold standard” in forensic toxicology.',
'A positive screen alone should not be treated as proof; confirmation is required.',
order('Toxicology testing process',['Collect specimens with chain of custody','Screen with immunoassay','Confirm with GC-MS or LC-MS','Quantify concentrations','Interpret in context of history and autopsy','Report findings'])]],
['Drug classes and effects',[
'**Opioids** (heroin, fentanyl, oxycodone, morphine) depress breathing and cause pinpoint pupils; overdose death results from **respiratory depression**. **Depressants** (alcohol, benzodiazepines, barbiturates) slow the central nervous system; combinations with opioids are especially dangerous. **Stimulants** (cocaine, methamphetamine, amphetamines) increase heart rate and blood pressure and can cause arrhythmias, stroke and hyperthermia.',
'**Hallucinogens** (LSD, psilocybin) alter perception; **dissociatives** (PCP, ketamine) cause detachment. **Cannabis** affects coordination and judgment. Prescription medications, such as antidepressants and heart drugs, can be toxic in overdose or interactions.',
'Know the key effects and the mechanism of death associated with each class.',
"Pharmacology concepts help interpret results: the **half-life** is the time for a drug’s concentration to fall by half; the **therapeutic range** is the concentration that produces benefit without toxicity; and **tolerance** means higher doses are needed for the same effect, so a level fatal to one person may be tolerated by another.",
sort('Drug class',['Opioid','Stimulant','Depressant'],[['Fentanyl',0],['Cocaine',1],['Alcohol',2],['Methamphetamine',1],['Oxycodone',0],['Benzodiazepine',2]])]],
['Alcohol',[
'**Ethanol** is the most commonly encountered drug in forensic cases. **Blood alcohol concentration (BAC)** is expressed in grams per deciliter; in the United States, **0.08%** is the legal driving limit for adults in most states. Effects range from impaired judgment at low levels to stupor, respiratory depression and death at very high levels, though tolerance varies.',
'Alcohol is absorbed mainly in the **small intestine**, distributed through body water and metabolized mostly by the **liver** at a fairly steady rate. In death investigations, alcohol may be a contributing condition (for example, in a fall or drowning) or a cause (acute alcohol poisoning). Vitreous humor testing helps confirm results when decomposition may have produced alcohol.',
'Combinations of alcohol with opioids or sedatives greatly increase the risk of fatal respiratory depression.',
flip('Alcohol facts',[['BAC units','Grams per deciliter (%)'],['U.S. driving limit','0.08% for adults in most states'],['Main absorption site','Small intestine'],['Main metabolism site','Liver'],['Vitreous testing','Confirms alcohol when decomposition is present'],['Dangerous combination','Alcohol with opioids or sedatives']])]],
['Carbon monoxide, cyanide and poisons',[
'**Carbon monoxide (CO)** is a colorless, odorless gas from fires, engines and faulty heaters. It binds hemoglobin far more strongly than oxygen, forming **carboxyhemoglobin** and causing tissue hypoxia. Findings include **cherry-red lividity** and elevated carboxyhemoglobin. In fire deaths, elevated carboxyhemoglobin and soot in the airways show the person was alive and breathing during the fire.',
'**Cyanide** blocks cells’ ability to use oxygen; it can be produced in fires from burning plastics. **Heavy metals** (arsenic, lead, mercury) and **pesticides** are other classic poisons. Toxicologists consider symptoms, scene clues (containers, prescriptions) and laboratory findings together.',
'Questions often ask about CO’s effect on hemoglobin and the significance of cherry-red lividity.',
"Scene evidence such as pill bottles, syringes, drug paraphernalia, notes and medical history helps toxicologists decide what to test for and how to interpret results. Investigators should collect medications found at the scene and record the number of pills remaining.",
match('Poison and key finding',[['Carbon monoxide','Carboxyhemoglobin and cherry-red lividity'],['Cyanide','Blocks cellular use of oxygen'],['Arsenic','Classic heavy metal poison'],['Opioid overdose','Respiratory depression'],['Stimulant overdose','Arrhythmia or hyperthermia'],['Fire victim breathing during fire','Soot in airways and elevated carboxyhemoglobin']])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Toxicology report',{a:{text:'An invented report shows fentanyl and a benzodiazepine in femoral blood, no injuries and a syringe at the scene. (Scenario invented for practice.) What is the likely immediate cause?',choices:[['Combined drug toxicity causing respiratory depression','b','Correct.',true],['Blunt force trauma','b','No injuries were found.',false]]},b:{text:'A screening test was positive for amphetamine, but GC-MS did not confirm it. How do you treat it?',choices:[['As a likely false positive; rely on the confirmed results','c','Correct.',true],['As proof of amphetamine use','c','Confirmation is required.',false]]},c:{text:'You cite the confirmed drugs, the scene evidence and the absence of injury in your conclusion.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Forensic toxicology detects and interprets drugs, alcohol and poisons; “the dose makes the poison,” and tolerance, disease and interactions matter.','Specimens: femoral blood (less redistribution), urine, vitreous humor, stomach contents, liver, hair; screen with immunoassay, confirm with GC-MS; consider postmortem redistribution and decomposition alcohol.','Opioids cause respiratory depression and pinpoint pupils; stimulants cause arrhythmias and hyperthermia; depressants slow the CNS; BAC 0.08% is the adult driving limit in most states; CO forms carboxyhemoglobin with cherry-red lividity; cyanide blocks oxygen use.'],terms:[['Forensic toxicology','The study of drugs and poisons for legal purposes.'],['Postmortem redistribution','Movement of drugs into blood after death.'],['Carboxyhemoglobin','Hemoglobin bound to carbon monoxide.']],mcqs:[q('Which test confirms a drug’s identity?','GC-MS','Immunoassay screen','Presumptive color test','Visual inspection','Gold standard.'),q('Which blood site is preferred for postmortem toxicology?','Femoral vein','Heart','Brain','Lung','Less redistribution.'),q('Which specimen shows long-term drug exposure?','Hair','Vitreous humor','Stomach contents','Fresh blood','Weeks to months.'),q('Opioid overdose death usually results from:','Respiratory depression','Hyperthermia','Bleeding','Seizures only','Breathing slows.'),q('Cocaine is a:','Stimulant','Opioid','Depressant','Hallucinogen','Increases heart rate.'),q('Cherry-red lividity suggests:','Carbon monoxide poisoning','Drowning','Hypothermia only','Opioids','Carboxyhemoglobin.'),q('The U.S. adult driving BAC limit in most states is:','0.08%','0.02%','0.15%','0.50%','Legal limit.'),q('Why might vitreous humor be tested for alcohol?','To confirm results when decomposition may produce alcohol','It is the only fluid with alcohol','It shows long-term use','It is easier than blood','Protected site.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how forensic toxicology contributes to death investigations, including specimens, testing methods and the interpretation of common findings.',rubric:[['Role',['drugs','alcohol','poisons','cause','contributing','dose','tolerance']],['Specimens',['femoral','urine','vitreous','stomach','liver','hair','redistribution']],['Testing',['screening','immunoassay','false positive','confirmatory','gc-ms']],['Interpretation',['opioid','respiratory','stimulant','alcohol','bac','carbon monoxide','carboxyhemoglobin']]],model:'Forensic toxicology detects and interprets drugs, alcohol and poisons to determine whether they caused or contributed to a death. Toxicologists remember that the dose makes the poison and that tolerance, age, disease and drug interactions affect how dangerous a concentration is. At autopsy, specimens are collected with chain of custody: femoral blood, which is less affected by postmortem redistribution than heart blood; urine to show past exposure; vitreous humor, a protected fluid useful for alcohol and electrolytes; stomach contents for recent ingestion; liver for metabolism; and hair for long-term exposure. Testing begins with screening tests such as immunoassays, which quickly detect drug classes but can give false positives, followed by confirmatory tests such as gas chromatography–mass spectrometry, which identify and measure specific substances. Interpretation connects findings to physiology and the scene. Opioids such as fentanyl cause death mainly by respiratory depression, especially when combined with alcohol or benzodiazepines, so a case with no injuries, a syringe and confirmed opioids points to drug toxicity as the cause. Stimulants such as cocaine can cause fatal arrhythmias or hyperthermia. A high blood alcohol concentration may be listed as a contributing condition in a fall or drowning. Carbon monoxide binds hemoglobin to form carboxyhemoglobin, producing cherry-red lividity, and elevated levels in a fire victim show the person was breathing during the fire, which helps determine manner.'},sources:[RULES,NIDA,MEDLINE_CO,NIAAA_BAC]};
